      "id": 1,
      "text": "Woodley Park takes its name from Woodley House, a 1801 mansion built by Philip Barton Key, the uncle of Francis Scott Key.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodley_Park_(Washington%2C_D.C.)"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Historic architecture lines the streets, while modern sidewalk cafés and restaurants keep the neighborhood buzzing with culinary charm.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://washington.org/dc-neighborhoods/woodley-park"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Woodley Park‑Zoo/Adams Morgan Metro station on the Red Line links the area to downtown and beyond.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodley_Park_(Washington%2C_D.C.)"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Leafy, green streets wind through the residential core, offering a serene escape from the city’s bustle.",
